Jumpa, jumpa, jumpa, jumpa, jumpa.

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:57.960
Some hot fizzle send to me soon.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:20:58.960
Ah, shucks.

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:00.000
That's why couts.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.000
He's had us on the stove since 4 o'clock in the morning.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.500
Get up, Jim.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:09.540
You can't get no hot fizzles until we come on wood to cook it with us.

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:19.940
Why don't you get out and walk?

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:22.120
It'll be a new experience for you.

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:30.000
God damn it, and we throw them together.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.300
Huh? He's gone back to her for more.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.360
Now, that shows he's kind of smitten by her, don't it?

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:40.700
Still mad?

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:45.170
Looking kind of tired.

00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:49.360
We just gotta get rid of her.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:51.960
You've got a long drive ahead of you.

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:56.530
You better let me drive your outfit for a while.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.700
If I need help from anybody, you...

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:02.210
You will be the last one I've lost.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:12.900
All right, Miss.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.340
Stagecoach!

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:22.140
Fancy, ain't she?

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:24.140
Must be making about six miles an hour.

00:22:24.140 --> 00:22:27.780
Yeah, and we ain't made over twelve miles all day.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:03.340
Plenty of water over there too.

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:04.360
See that smoke?

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:06.560
Stagecoach must have camped by now.

00:23:06.700 --> 00:23:07.900
And we'll camp there too.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.360
Come on, let's lope over and surprise.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:10.420
Come on, Bill.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:11.040
Come on.

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:12.760
Got to camp.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:15.650
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:28.060
Engine.

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:29.890
Stagecoach.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.360
I didn't figure on him so soon.

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:04.470
Didn't know there were any on the warpath this side of the post.

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:07.150
They won't jump a big outfit like this, will they?

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:08.960
Can't never tell.

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:10.900
Think we ought to push on to the post?

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:18.180
Cavalry.

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:20.680
Sailors caught us in.

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:41.690
- I'm glad to see you. - What's happened?

00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:44.480
- I was jumped the mail stage. I thought you knew it.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:47.380
- You thought we knew? - Yes.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:50.320
Didn't you come out to escort us back to the post?

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:52.000
We're on our way to the war.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.920
President Lincoln has sent for all the Frontier troops.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.380
You mean to say there ain't no more troops at the post?

00:24:56.380 --> 00:24:59.380
As far as I know, we're the last company between here and Santa Fe.

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:02.730
- Then you ain't helping us.

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:05.170
- Sorry, we're joining Grant's army at Vicksburg.

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:07.370
- What does Lincoln and the government think they're doing?

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:09.370
Stripping this western territory of protection.

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:12.660
- Yeah, Braden's the party they gotta go on.

00:25:12.660 --> 00:25:14.120
War or no war?

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:15.360
- Maybe you haven't heard

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:18.220
that the Confederates got pretty near to Washington.

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:19.860
- No, we've been too busy out here

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:23.560
to pay any attention to that picayune squabble back east.

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:27.750
- Hey Jim, maybe this is old Chance.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:28.930
- Chance?

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:33.540
Hi, in just a minute.

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:35.980
Look here, coach.

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:39.390
Engines is going to be mighty dangerous from now on.

00:25:40.780 --> 00:25:43.240
Why don't you send the women folk back with the soldiers, huh?

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:47.630
If they want to go, you'll escort them as far as independence, won't you?

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:49.340
I can do that, yes.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:53.140
Bill Jackson, you got fox blood into you somewheres.

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:53.940
Shut up.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:56.290
Where Tom goes, I go.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:59.860
He gets into trouble, I get in it with him.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:07.370
how about her she ain't got nobody belongs to her in this year outfit that's right send me back

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:16.000
no i would not go back they are not afraid to go on neither am i i said i would go to sacramento

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:24.170
and i'm going on oh what do you think we are you got get up and get to you now what are we gonna do

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:29.630
crash see i don't think your train ought to risk it folks in sacramento are expecting

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:31.000
- Need any ammunition?

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:32.090
- Got 20.

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:33.170
Well, good luck to you.

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:55.990
- Thank you, Captain.

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:58.180
- They took the stagehorses with them.

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.000
The rest of the hoof prints was on shore

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:03.270
The signs look like as if they went southeast, about 50 in the band.

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:06.150
That little band wouldn't try to jump this outfit, would they?

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:10.370
That may have only been a part of a big band, see?

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:14.040
Suppose we prowl around and find out if they're joining up with other bands.

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:15.780
You think it'd be safe to bed down here?

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:17.600
Sure, Indians won't jump us before dawn.

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.000
Good, you better get started.

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:23.250
All right, men, let's curl up our wagons and bed down here for the night.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:24.420
All right, come on.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:42.290
Shucks, you don't have to worry about it.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:46.030
I understand that Indians don't have to use blondes.

00:27:46.230 --> 00:27:48.630
Shut up, will you? You're not so funny.

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:52.720
- What's that? - It must be them.

00:27:57.730 --> 00:27:59.890
Well, what did you find out?

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:02.430
The engines have rolled off, ain't they?

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:05.730
Yes, so they can band together with more of them

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:07.030
for an attack on us.

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:08.740
They were signaling.

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.140
You better break up, comp, right now.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.440
Yeah, and try to get to the post.

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:14.440
The engines won't strike before dawn.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:16.450
We'll leave the campfires burning,

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:17.750
so they'll think we're still here.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:20.750
You're in an old fired hurry to get to the post, ain't you?

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.760
That's the reason you kept back worried about the wood being here.

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:25.960
So you can make us get to where you can do some more

00:28:26.060 --> 00:28:27.860
of your drinking and carousing.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:28.860
Hold on.

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:30.000
When I tell you to get back,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.520
get to go in, they know what they're talking about.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.380
I'll get your stock hitched up.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:36.100
And don't forget to throw ashes on them graves.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:39.190
And then drive your wagons across them like I told you.

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:42.280
What's that?

00:28:43.580 --> 00:28:43.820
What?

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:44.410
That.

00:28:44.810 --> 00:28:45.490
Oh, that.

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:49.370
What's nothing, just a couple of scalps.

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:50.410
Why, you...

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:53.750
He swore he's going to have a hundred of them by the time he's 70.

00:28:53.750 --> 00:28:55.030
You attacked the Indians?

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:59.030
Yeah, they had them stagecoach horses with them.

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:01.480
You run that risk?

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:02.480
I know, coach.

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:05.470
There was only 10 or 12 men in the room with them horses.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:08.470
You jeopardized the lives of everyone in this outfit.

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.620
For six worthless horses?

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:11.620
Worthless horses?

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:15.860
They'll be worth $40 apiece to us when we get to Sacramento.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:18.810
Always thinking about yourselves.

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:19.810
Oh, coach.

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:20.810
Look here.

00:29:20.810 --> 00:29:21.800
Come on.

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:29.670
Hello there.

00:29:35.530 --> 00:29:39.500
Got a toothache?

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:43.800
If you have, why, Seth can take it out for you with his pliers.

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:46.070
He's kind of handy with things like that.

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:48.870
Used to be a blacksmith.

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:52.680
Oh, no.

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.710
Well, then.

00:29:54.710 --> 00:29:56.820
I'm afraid.

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:59.090
I thought it was funny he wasn't scared before.

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:04.630
What did you start for California for anyway? Alone.

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:06.750
I do not start alone.

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:07.450
Who?

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:15.190
I start together with my father, but on our way from Vincent, he goes into his bed.

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:19.760
For five weeks I watch, but he died.

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:21.170
I'm sorry.

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:24.460
But he said to me before, go on.

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:27.990
I guess he didn't know what going on would mean.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:30.000
No, he do not know.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:37.420
- I hear there are lots of French people in Indiana.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:40.180
- Yes, we are all French at Van Sant.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:41.960
- I know you've had a tough time,

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:43.580
and I hope it's all behind you

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:46.520
when you get out to California.

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:48.490
- I hope so.

00:30:48.490 --> 00:30:54.300
But now, oh, I'm a big, how you say, turkey?

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:56.570
- Turkey?

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:58.780
I don't know what you're aiming at.

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.000
- Yes, because I have the fear.

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:01.910
I'm stupid. I'm turkey.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:03.240
Turkey?

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:14.500
Yes. When a woman make herself silly and how you say it, uh, oh, goose, maybe, hmm?

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:17.060
That's it. Goose?

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:17.520
Yeah.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:18.900
But you're not a goose.

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:19.620
Huh?

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:24.170
You're as brave as anybody. Most of us are as shaky as you are tonight.

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:29.920
And, uh, us men get our courage out of bottles and all the other women have somebody to lean on.

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.120
- But you've got nobody.

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:35.460
- That's why you come and talk to me,

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:38.360
so I can lean on you a little while.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:40.840
- Well, maybe.

00:31:40.840 --> 00:31:42.300
Anyhow, I didn't come over

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:44.920
because I thought you was good and mad at me.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:47.500
- I'm not mad now.

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:51.530
- Well then, don't be scared anymore.

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:53.630
We're moving to the post on the cover of night.

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:55.300
You'll be all right.

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:57.150
- I'm all right now.

00:31:57.150 --> 00:31:59.830
And I will not be afraid again, I promise.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:04.190
- What do you say when you mean something very much?

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.690
- Shake.

00:32:31.790 --> 00:32:33.940
- Let's go!

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:24.590
- They've been hitting it up like this.

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:25.530
- Most of the rigs now,

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:27.740
ever since the wagon train got rained in.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:30.000
- Yeah, like a lot of sailors getting in the port, huh?

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:33.610
Well, a bottle of whiskey will cost you a barrel of flour.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:37.290
Why, that's the same as robbing a man.

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:38.490
It suits me, brother.

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:40.290
You can take it or leave.

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.690
Well, all right.

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:45.570
Jerry, a bottle of bourbon.

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:49.500
But do you see what I see?

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:54.180
Jeff!

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:58.040
Jeff Muppet, you're gross!

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:00.000
You're gross!

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:03.950
You old son of a rattlesnake!

00:34:03.950 --> 00:34:06.830
- Howdy Jim! - And Bill Jackson!

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:08.580
- Yee! - Ha ha ha!

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:12.130
- Newman, what are you doing in this skunk pool?

00:34:12.130 --> 00:34:14.180
- Skill scouting? - Scouting?

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:17.570
Not me. I'm working for the railroad.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:22.280
- Railroad? What railroad? - The Atchison, Topeka, Santa Fe.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:30.000
- Putting our railroad through when they can't hardly get a wagon. - Hey, they give up that fool idea way back in '58.

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.420
Well, it's on again.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:34.910
These two fellows with me are surveying it now.

00:34:34.910 --> 00:34:38.550
Say, in a few years, you won't need no scouts.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:41.550
They'll always need scouts, old son of a guy.

00:34:41.550 --> 00:34:42.550
You're a star, come on.

00:34:42.550 --> 00:34:43.550
Get your head out.

00:34:43.550 --> 00:34:46.550
How about it?

00:34:46.550 --> 00:34:48.250
Stock's in plain shape.

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:50.650
We can travel all right if you want to,

00:34:50.650 --> 00:34:52.150
but I'd rather wait a couple of days.

00:34:52.150 --> 00:34:54.070
The ground's still pretty spongy.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:55.580
Ordinarily, I would, Clint.

00:34:55.580 --> 00:34:57.580
But I got to get these hob-raising independents

00:34:57.580 --> 00:35:00.000
out here 'fore they all go plum loco.

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:01.850
Why don't you pull out without them independents?

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.600
No account of a drunken bunch of cattle, anyhow.

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:05.580
Can't do that.

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.180
I need them for protection against the engines.

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:10.080
You got 18 company wagons, ain't you?

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:10.940
Yes.

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:14.080
Nothing out there but small bands of engines now.

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:16.780
But if you ever find out the soldiers is gone,

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:20.040
they're going to band together in one big war party.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:23.200
Better leave while they're still scattered.

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:25.890
How do you know so much about it?

00:35:25.890 --> 00:35:28.440
I was out there in the Indian country about two months ago

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:30.000
with an immigrant train that was massacred

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:38.280
You got away from here, but I was the only one they don't because we only had six out there

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:45.940
But they ain't gonna start no trouble with 18 out it. I still wouldn't favor splitting the train

00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:51.250
The couch I've about come to the end of my tether we got to get out of here

00:35:51.640 --> 00:36:00.000
Mr. Carl why can we leave this terrible place? Everybody has lost their mind stupid people gambling and drinking fighting

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:03.860
I know, ma'am, but I ain't got any control over these independent freighters.

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:06.530
Oh, but look at this poor woman.

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:09.580
A husband is drunk, playing car,

00:36:10.110 --> 00:36:11.490
gave me all their goods away.

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:13.680
Another man has lost his own wagon.

00:36:13.960 --> 00:36:15.940
I'm just as anxious to go as you are, ma'am.

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:18.710
But I can't until I round up these independents.

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:21.160
I'll tell you one way you can round them up.

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:21.640
How?

00:36:22.190 --> 00:36:25.980
You've got enough company drivers that ain't two drunklers too sober to fight.

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:28.530
You've got me and Bridger and Jackson.

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:30.000
You say the word anyway.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.030
We'll handle them.

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:34.350
My gosh, that's a good idea.

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:35.810
They've gathered up your drivers.

00:36:35.810 --> 00:36:37.930
Wait, have them hook up their team so when we get them

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:39.330
rounded up, we can put them in the wagons

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:40.450
and get started pronto.

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:42.430
That'll mean hitching up the independent teams too.

00:36:42.430 --> 00:36:42.930
That's right.

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:44.850
Oh, don't you worry, Mr. Couch.

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:47.100
Us women folks will tend to that.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:49.300
My girls will kitchen and help too.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.250
Them with their sheltered knives.

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.500
Time they had a few pallets on their hands.

00:36:53.500 --> 00:36:57.960
They're getting plumped, demoralized.

00:36:57.960 --> 00:37:00.000
I'll get a hold of Bridger and Jackson.

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:17.960
What is it saying?

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:23.260
They want to know if they can buy our fire engine to make fire water.

00:37:23.260 --> 00:37:29.490
Look at her. A wiggling and a pouting and a buttoning up with that rubber motha hern.

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:34.920
just like a snake of putting a spell on a bird.

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.740
He ain't been himself for the last three days.

00:37:37.740 --> 00:37:40.280
I've been watching him pretty close.

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:44.010
Yesterday, he only had 11 drinks.

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:45.750
And he's been a working, too.

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:51.230
But doing things for Couchy, he don't have to do.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:54.790
She's got him all right.

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:57.260
Let's get drunk.

00:37:57.260 --> 00:37:58.710
Well, let's try anyway.

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:10.300
Just a bottle of that whiskey.

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:13.680
No, no, I'll get you a bottle of 20-year-old Kentucky bourbon.

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:16.840
No, no, we'll drink this bar whiskey.

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.480
It's got more herb to it.

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:39.540
Well, what of it?

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:41.970
What do you mean?

